      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "PART I: Allogeneic transplantation",
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 16,
      "target": "../sections/sec-016-part-i-allogeneic-transplantation.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Operative paragraph 2.1 \n \n86. \nREQUESTS the Director-General: to continue examining and collecting global data \non the practices, safety, quality, efficacy and epidemiology of allogeneic transplantation and \non ethical issues, including living donation, in order to update the Guiding principles on \nhuman organ transplantation.1 \n \n87. \nAllogeneic transplantation entails transplantation of human organs (e.g. kidney, liver, \nheart, lung, skin, cornea) and tissues (including human cells such as haematopoietic stem \ncells from bone marrow, peripheral blood or cord blood) to other humans to restor",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes plans under WHA57.18 to collect data on the practice, safety and ethics of allogeneic transplantation of organs, tissues and cells to update WHO guiding principles. It notes the limited availability of such services in the region and plans to disseminate information to support regional cooperation.",
